{"content":"Grass appears green because of the chlorophyll, a pigment found in the leaves of plants. Chlorophyll is essential for photosynthesis, the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy. Chlorophyll absorbs light primarily in the blue and red parts of the light spectrum and reflects green light, which is why grass and other plants appear green to our eyes.\n\nIn more detail, when sunlight hits the leaves of grass, the chlorophyll molecules absorb the blue and red wavelengths of light and use this energy to drive the photosynthesis process. The green light is not absorbed as efficiently and is instead reflected back to our eyes, making the grass appear green. This is why green is the dominant color we see in grass and other plants.","level":"info","message":"Inference Client response:","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[]}
